digraph ReasoningTree {
  // paper_id=s41467-025-56106-3
  rankdir="TB";
  "NROOT" [label="(0,0,0) Develop and evaluate an integrated solar-driven seawater photoelectrochemical device using tandem BiVO4 photoanodes and Si photocathodes in boundary-layer shear flow to generate CO2(aq) in situ from bicarbonate, deliver the molecular flux to photocathodes, and test whether this non-bubbling architecture improves dissolved-inorganic-carbon conversion to gaseous fuels.", fillcolor="lightyellow", style="filled"];
  "NROOT_COMMON" [label="(0,0,0) When seawater provides abundant dissolved inorganic carbon but static PEC suffers low active CO2(aq), pH/transport losses, and impractical CO2 bubbling, supported in situ CO2 generation and boundary-layer flow delivery motivate an integrated solar-driven PEC design to be developed and evaluated."];
  "R16" [label="(0,0,0) Where diffusion-dominant operation creates large pH gradients and reduces local CO2(aq), PEC CO2R selectivity and efficiency are expected to be poor."];
  "R18" [label="(0,0,0) Given near-zero active CO2(aq) in seawater, in situ CO2(aq) generation from bicarbonate with rapid delivery to the cathode is a plausible strategy to reduce reliance on external CO2 bubbling."];
  "R19" [label="(0,0,0) The expected lower energy and economic advantages of integrated PEC, together with practical challenges in stepwise capture-plus-conversion, motivate developing integrated direct-utilization PEC."];
  "R24" [label="(0,0,0) The demonstrated boundary-layer molecular-flux catalysis regime supports testing whether boundary-flow-coupled PEC can convert low seawater DIC into a controlled CO2(aq) flux at catalysts."];
  "R26" [label="(0,0,0) Seawater’s multi-species composition and equilibria motivate considering protective or selective coatings for practical direct ocean PEC operation."];
  "R27" [label="(0,0,0) The molecular-flux catalysis concept motivates exploration of solar-driven conversion or improved utilization of dilute reactants beyond the specific demonstrated seawater case."];
  "R28" [label="(0,0,0) Supported in situ CO2(aq) generation and boundary-layer delivery/loss mitigation provide a design context for an integrated boundary-layer molecular-flux PEC concept."];
  "R29" [label="(0,0,0) The motivation to pursue lower-energy capture/conversion routes together with the need for non-bubbling reactant supply supports integrated and non-bubbling design goals."];
  "R3" [label="(0,0,0) Under conditions where PEC CO2R has insufficient CO2(aq), CO2(aq) availability is an important constraint on forming gaseous carbon products such as CO and C2H4."];
  "R32" [label="(0,0,0) Because direct ocean CO2 conversion faces seawater multi-species challenges, chemically selective coatings are a motivated design feature for passing desired reactants while limiting poisoning."];
  "R33" [label="(0,0,0) If sustained flow is required for boundary-layer transport, possible tidal or ocean-current energy harvesting supports the feasibility of boundary-layer-flow operation in ocean deployment."];
  "R34" [label="(0,0,0) Boundary-layer transport control and seawater-compatible selective coatings are supported design considerations for ocean PEC systems."];
  "R35" [label="(0,0,0) Boundary-layer transport control and possible tidal or ocean-current flow energy strengthen, but do not prove, operational feasibility for boundary-layer-flow ocean PEC systems."];
  "R36" [label="(0,0,0) Combining boundary-layer flow, in situ CO2 generation, and seawater compatibility is a plausible integrated ocean PEC architecture, with operational feasibility strengthened by flow and protection considerations."];
  "R37" [label="(0,0,0) A non-bubbling reactant-supply need and feasible in situ CO2(aq) generation support treating these as candidate components of an integrated ocean PEC architecture."];
  "R38" [label="(0,0,0) The evidence supports in situ CO2(aq) generation from seawater bicarbonate and motivates a non-bubbling reactant-supply strategy."];
  "R39" [label="(0,0,0) A plausible integrated ocean PEC architecture can include in situ CO2 generation as one supported element, without proving that the full design set is complete."];
  "R40" [label="(0,0,0) The plausible combination of boundary-layer flow, in situ CO2 generation, and seawater compatibility motivates using an integrated ocean PEC architecture as a research direction to investigate."];
  "R41" [label="(0,0,0) Photoanode-enabled in situ CO2(aq) generation and boundary-layer flow delivery/loss mitigation are supported elements of the current proposal context."];
  "R42" [label="(0,0,0) A demonstrated boundary-layer-flow PEC architecture together with the need for non-bubbling reactant supply motivates investigating a non-bubbling seawater PEC approach."];
  "R43" [label="(0,0,0) The experimentally realizable boundary-layer-flow PEC concept and the impracticality of CO2 bubbling offshore motivate a non-bubbling seawater PEC research approach."];
  "R44" [label="(0,0,0) The supported needs and component mechanisms motivate proposing an integrated solar-driven ocean PEC device that generates CO2(aq) in situ from seawater bicarbonate and uses boundary-layer flow to deliver that flux to photocathodes while addressing transport losses and seawater compatibility."];
  "R5" [label="(0,0,0) During diffusional transport, acid-base buffering, pH gradients, and ion transport are implicated as contributors to major transport losses between anode and cathode."];
  "R7" [label="(0,0,0) Because seawater has low active dissolved carbon availability and HCO3− is not considered electrochemically active for CO2R, approaches that increase available CO2(aq) from bicarbonate are motivated."];
  "nI1" [label="(0,0,0) If a CO2 capture/conversion pathway is too energy-intensive/costly, then alternative feedstocks/processes with higher carbon availability or lower separation work should be pursued."];
  "nI11" [label="(0,0,0) induction: Given diffusion creates strong pH gradients and CO2(aq) is consumed by OH− near cathodes, therefore diffusion-dominant operation commonly leads to CO2(aq) depletion near cathodes in buffered electrolytes."];
  "nI12a" [label="(0,0,0) deduction: Given if electrolyte is static then diffusion dominates and currently conventional PEC operates in static electrolyte, therefore conventional PEC is diffusion-dominant."];
  "nI14" [label="(0,0,0) abduction: Given CO2 bubbling is costly/not viable and currently scaling bubbling offshore is impractical, therefore a non-bubbling reactant-supply strategy is needed."];
  "nI15" [label="(0,0,0) deduction: Given if convection dominates then boundary-layer control can reduce diffusion-driven pH gradients and maintain reactants and currently flow can replace diffusion as dominant transport, therefore a flow/boundary-layer design can mitigate pH/transport losses and CO2(aq) depletion."];
  "nI16" [label="(0,0,0) deduction: Given if HCO3− is acidified with H+ then CO2(aq) can be generated and currently BiVO4 photoanode generates H+ and acidifies HCO3−, therefore photoanode-generated H+ can produce CO2(aq) from seawater bicarbonate."];
  "nI17" [label="(0,0,0) deduction: Given if boundary-layer shear confines near-surface CO2(aq) flux and if flow prevents out-diffusion while increasing transport flux, therefore boundary-layer flow enables efficient delivery of anode-generated CO2(aq) to downstream cathodes without bulk loss."];
  "nI19" [label="(0,0,0) deduction: Given if boundary-layer flow reduces pH gradients and concentration-limited transport losses then PEC efficiency improves and currently pH gradients/transport losses likely limit PEC performance, therefore applying boundary-layer flow should raise PEC efficiency for CO2R/water splitting."];
  "nI19a" [label="(0,0,0) abduction: Given high pH is observed near CO2R cathodes and CO2R/H2 evolution consumes H+ and produces OH−, therefore OH− generation and H+ consumption likely contribute to cathode alkalinization that worsens CO2(aq) availability."];
  "nI1m" [label="(0,0,0) deduction: Given if CCUS is crucial and if costly pathways should be replaced by better ones, therefore pursuing alternative lower-energy capture/conversion routes is motivated."];
  "nI2" [label="(0,0,0) If a medium contains much higher inorganic carbon concentration than air and is abundant, then it is a promising target for large-scale CO2 sourcing/capture."];
  "nI22" [label="(0,0,0) If an integrated ocean PEC system can (i) generate CO2(aq) in situ from HCO3− using photoanode H+, (ii) confine/deliver a CO2(aq) molecular flux via boundary-layer flow to cathodes to avoid depletion and reduce pH-gradient losses, (iii) avoid CO2 bubbling, (iv) incorporate selective coatings for seawater impurities, and (v) leverage ocean/tidal energy for flow, then it is a viable research direction for scalable solar-driven direct ocean CO2 conversion."];
  "nI23b" [label="(0,0,0) deduction: Given if pH-swing acidification shifts DIC toward CO2(aq) and currently photoanode-generated H+ acidifies HCO3− to CO2(aq), therefore in situ CO2(aq) generation from seawater bicarbonate is feasible."];
  "nI23c" [label="(0,0,0) deduction: Given if flow-dominant boundary-layer control mitigates diffusion-driven pH gradients and maintains reactants and currently boundary-layer shear confines/delivers CO2(aq) flux, therefore boundary-layer flow can address CO2(aq) depletion and transport losses."];
  "nI23d" [label="(0,0,0) deduction: Given if operating in seawater then selective coatings are needed to pass reactants and prevent poisoning and currently seawater composition implies poisoning risk, therefore include selective coatings in the ocean PEC design."];
  "nI24" [label="(0,0,0) If a design requires sustained flow for boundary-layer transport, then availability of renewable mechanical energy (tides/currents) supports feasibility in ocean deployment."];
  "nI3" [label="(0,0,0) If local CO2(aq) near a CO2R cathode is depleted and pH gradients are large, then selectivity and efficiency of PEC CO2R will be poor."];
  "nI30" [label="(0,0,0) induction: Given boundary-layer flow can be powered by tides/currents and transport/protection components are supported, therefore operational feasibility is commonly strengthened for boundary-layer-flow ocean PEC systems."];
  "nI31" [label="(0,0,0) Currently, combining boundary-layer flow, in situ CO2 generation, and seawater compatibility yields a plausible integrated ocean PEC architecture."];
  "nI32" [label="(0,0,0) If an architecture is coherent and addresses the identified bottlenecks (CO2(aq) depletion, pH losses, bubbling impracticality), then it can serve as the immediate basis for the research proposal."];
  "nI38a" [label="(0,0,0) induction: Given typical practice is stepwise acidify then bubble CO2 and currently stepwise capture+feed to PEC exists, therefore stepwise capture+conversion is an established approach for seawater-to-fuels."];
  "nI4" [label="(0,0,0) If a process can generate CO2(aq) in situ from bicarbonate at/near the anode and deliver it rapidly to the cathode, then the need for external CO2 bubbling can be reduced/eliminated."];
  "nI43a" [label="(0,0,0) deduction: Given currently new methods are motivated to manage diffusion losses and currently flowing-seawater PEC without bubbling is unexplored, therefore exploring flow-based PEC designs is a timely direction."];
  "nI49a" [label="(0,0,0) induction: Given molecular-flux catalysis in boundary-layer flow is demonstrated and currently a custom 3D-printed PEC device generates such boundary-layer flow, therefore boundary-layer-flow PEC architectures are experimentally realizable."];
  "nI5" [label="(0,0,0) If convection dominates transport in a PEC cell, then boundary-layer control can reduce diffusion-driven pH gradients and maintain reactant availability near electrodes."];
  "nI5a" [label="(0,0,0) deduction: Given if PEC devices can be powered by sunlight and currently PEC uses photoanode/photocathode pairs with sunlight as input, therefore sunlight-only PEC can in principle drive water oxidation and fuel-forming reactions."];
  "nI6" [label="(0,0,0) If seawater impurities can poison catalysts, then protective/selective coatings are a necessary design element for practical ocean operation."];
  "nI60a" [label="(0,0,0) deduction: Given if molecular flux is controlled via flow velocity then selectivity can be tuned and if boundary-flow-coupled PEC converts low DIC to CO2(aq) flux, therefore flow control can tune selectivity while sustaining reactant flux from dilute bicarbonate."];
  "nI7" [label="(0,0,0) deduction: Given if a pathway is too energy-intensive/costly then pursue alternatives and currently DAC is energy-intensive and costly, therefore alternatives to DAC should be pursued."];
  "nI7b" [label="(0,0,0) deduction: Given if a medium has much higher inorganic carbon and is abundant then it is promising and currently seawater is commonly a promising target for CO2 sourcing, therefore seawater is a promising CO2 source for CCUS/solar fuels."];
  "nI8" [label="(0,0,0) induction: Given seawater has much higher inorganic carbon than air and seawater is a significant net sink with large-scale potential, therefore seawater is commonly a promising target for large-scale CO2 sourcing/capture."];
  "nI9" [label="(0,0,0) abduction: Given low STF efficiencies are observed and currently large pH-dependent potential losses can occur, therefore pH gradients/transport losses likely limit PEC performance."];
  "nR3_7" [label="(4,3,7) If a pH-swing shifts solution to acidic pH, then dissolved inorganic carbon species shift toward CO2(aq), enabling CO2 capture/release."];
  "nS1" [label="(1,0,0) Currently, developing carbon capture, utilization, and storage (CCUS) technologies is crucial to manage anthropogenic CO2 emissions and supply sustainable chemicals/fuels."];
  "nS10" [label="(10,0,0) Currently, stepwise capture+conversion is rarely demonstrated because of multiple practical challenges."];
  "nS11" [label="(11,0,0) If capture and conversion are integrated in one PEC process directly using dissolved carbon, then it is expected to use much less energy and be more economically viable than stepwise PV-driven capture + electrolysis."];
  "nS12" [label="(12,0,0) Currently, a conventional unbiased PEC CO2R + water oxidation device operates in a static electrolyte."];
  "nS15" [label="(15,0,0) If the electrolyte is static, then diffusion dominates transport and ions (H+, HCO3−, etc.) carry ionic current."];
  "nS16" [label="(16,0,0) Currently, acid-base reactions occur during diffusional transport of CO2(aq), H+, and HCO3−."];
  "nS17" [label="(17,0,0) Currently, major transport losses come from pH gradients, ion transport, and acid-base buffer reactions between anode and cathode."];
  "nS19" [label="(19,0,0) Currently, CO2R/H2 evolution consume H+ and net produce OH−."];
  "nS2" [label="(2,0,0) Currently, direct air capture (DAC) is energy-intensive and costly."];
  "nS21" [label="(21,0,0) Currently, diffusion-dominant transport creates a strong pH gradient (e.g., pH 10→4) that raises cathode pH and reduces local CO2(aq)."];
  "nS22" [label="(22,0,0) Currently, the anode–cathode pH difference can add up to ~500 mV potential loss due to pH-dependent thermodynamics."];
  "nS23" [label="(23,0,0) Currently, STF efficiency for PEC CO2R/water splitting is low (examples 0.08%, 0.43%, 0.63%), consistent with major losses."];
  "nS3" [label="(3,0,0) Currently, seawater dissolved inorganic carbon is ~140× higher (as bicarbonate) than atmospheric CO2 (~420 ppm)."];
  "nS31" [label="(31,0,0) Currently, diffusion transport of H+ and HCO3− in buffered electrolyte leads to high pH near CO2R cathodes."];
  "nS32" [label="(32,0,0) Currently, CO2(aq) is constantly consumed by OH− near the cathode."];
  "nS33" [label="(33,0,0) Currently, there is insufficient CO2(aq) reactant for PEC CO2R under these conditions."];
  "nS35" [label="(35,0,0) Currently, seawater use is challenged by low concentrations of dissolved carbon species (HCO3−, CO2(aq))."];
  "nS36" [label="(36,0,0) Currently, HCO3− is not considered an electrochemically active species for (photo-)electrochemical CO2R."];
  "nS37" [label="(37,0,0) Currently, seawater has only ~2.3 mM bicarbonate and electrochemically active CO2(aq) is near zero."];
  "nS38" [label="(38,0,0) Currently, typical seawater-to-solar-fuel practice is: acidify seawater to convert HCO3− to CO2, then continuously bubble CO2 into a conventional PEC device to raise reactant concentration."];
  "nS4" [label="(4,0,0) Currently, seawater is a ~0.4 Gt CO2/yr net sink and could support trillion-ton-scale CO2 capture/utilization/sequestration via engineering."];
  "nS40" [label="(40,0,0) Currently, continuous CO2 bubbling is costly, has low energy/carbon utilization (incl. CO2–OH− loss), and is not economically viable."];
  "nS41" [label="(41,0,0) Currently, scaling CO2 bubbling to large-area ocean-sited PEC reactors is impractical."];
  "nS42" [label="(42,0,0) Currently, challenges motivate new methods to manage diffusion-based transport losses and CO2(aq) depletion near photocathodes."];
  "nS43" [label="(43,0,0) Currently, PEC devices leveraging flowing seawater (2.3 mM HCO3−) without CO2 bubbling remain largely unexplored."];
  "nS44" [label="(44,0,0) If seawater is flowing in the device, then convection/flow can replace diffusion as the dominant transport mechanism."];
  "nS45" [label="(45,0,0) Currently, seawater’s multi-species composition/equilibria (Na+, Cl−, Mg2+, Ca2+, organics, microbes) challenges direct ocean CO2 capture/conversion."];
  "nS46" [label="(46,0,0) If operating in seawater, then chemical selective coatings are needed to pass desired reactants while preventing catalyst poisoning by minerals/impurities."];
  "nS48" [label="(48,0,0) Currently, an understudied regime is demonstrated: molecular-flux catalysis where CO2(aq) reactants continuously flow to catalysts within a boundary layer flow."];
  "nS49" [label="(49,0,0) Currently, boundary-layer flow is generated by a custom 3D-printed PEC device using tandem BiVO4 photoanodes and Si photocathodes arranged in parallel."];
  "nS5" [label="(5,0,0) If a PEC device is used, then sunlight alone can power converting dissolved carbon in seawater into fuels/chemicals."];
  "nS50" [label="(50,0,0) If PEC CO2R catalysis is coupled with boundary-flow transport, then low seawater DIC (2.3 mM HCO3−) can be converted into a flux of CO2(aq)."];
  "nS52" [label="(52,0,0) If there is boundary-layer shear flow, then it confines a near-surface continuous CO2(aq) molecular flux from photoanode to photocathode."];
  "nS53" [label="(53,0,0) If BiVO4 photoanode operates under sunlight in flowing seawater, then it generates H+ and acidifies HCO3−."];
  "nS54" [label="(54,0,0) If flow is present, then it prevents CO2(aq) out-diffusion to bulk, increases CO2(aq)/H+ transport flux, and reduces residence time to improve utilization downstream."];
  "nS55" [label="(55,0,0) If needed, flow energy can be harvested from tides/ocean currents, making boundary-layer-flow operation feasible on the ocean."];
  "nS58" [label="(58,0,0) If boundary-layer flow is used, then pH gradients and concentration-limited transport losses are reduced, improving PEC efficiency for CO2R/water splitting broadly."];
  "nS6" [label="(6,0,0) If HCO3− is acidified with H+ (HCO3− + H+ → CO2(aq) + H2O), then dissolved CO2(aq) can be generated on demand in seawater."];
  "nS60" [label="(60,0,0) If molecular flux is controlled via flow velocity, then CO2R selectivity can be tuned."];
  "nS64" [label="(64,0,0) Currently, the molecular-flux catalysis concept could enable solar-driven direct conversion of low-concentration reactants or improve utilization efficiency of dilute reactants."];
  "nS7" [label="(7,0,0) If CO2(aq) is available, then CO2 reduction (CO2R) can convert it into gaseous carbon products (e.g., CO, C2H4) for downstream liquid fuel/chemical synthesis."];
  "nS8" [label="(8,0,0) Currently, PEC devices use photoanode/photocathode pairs to drive water oxidation (anode) and fuel-forming CO2R (cathode) using sunlight as the only energy input."];
  "nS9" [label="(9,0,0) Currently, a stepwise method can capture CO2 from seawater dissolved carbon and then feed it to PEC CO2R conversion."];
